\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Implications for Benchmark Temporal Comparability}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

The 31\% ETS~C instability rate is robust across thresholds (10--49\% at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \in [2.5, 1.0]$) and confirmed by structural controls (\Cref{sec:structural}). The temporal cohort ability gap is negligible ($d = -0.17$, overlap $= 76.4\%$); ability-matched C\% of $28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$ confirms the 31.3\% rate is not inflated by impact confounding. Domain accuracy improvement explains only 2.4 pp of 31.3\% C\%; 92.4\% is item-specific. This does not invalidate MMLU as a ranking instrument ($\rho \approx 0.997$) but signals measurement drift across model generations.

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:disc_groundtruth}

The near-independence between DIF flags and web-overlap labels (enrichment $= 0.964$, Jaccard $= 0.206$, $\phi = 0.012$; consistent across contamination subtypes) exposes a broader gap: the dominant validation practice of evaluating contamination detectors against web-overlap labels conflates global exposure with differential functioning. Most MMLU items appear in web text, so web-overlap labels identify a condition shared by nearly all items. This near-independence is the expected outcome when a differential method is evaluated against a global-exposure reference. Item-level differential detectors require item-level differential ground truth.

\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}
\label{sec:disc_diagnostic}

The GSM1K correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$; \Cref{sec:gsm8k}) provides exploratory evidence that DIF-flagged items capture behavioral change distinct from memorization. Models with larger contamination gaps benefit \emph{less} from DIF-C items. Of the 249 DIF-C items on GSM8K, 171 favor the reference (2023) cohort ($\alpha_{\mathrm{MH}} > 1$). Partial correlation ($r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$) after controlling for accuracy confirms the effect is not an artifact of general model quality. Caveats include a wide confidence interval (95\% CI $= [-0.71, -0.16]$) and limited sample size.

Item characterization reinforces this: DIF-C items are harder ($d = -0.247$) and more discriminating ($d = 0.230$), distributed uniformly across subjects ($\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$). A difficulty $\times$ direction interaction ($\rho = -0.597$, $p < 0.001$) shows capability gains concentrate on hard items (88.7\% focal-favoring in hardest decile) while easy items erode (96.2\% reference-favoring in easiest decile).

Yen's Q3 analysis confirms that local dependence does not drive DIF: extreme LD correlates negligibly with subject-level C\% ($r = 0.09$, $p = 0.50$), while reasoning-heavy domains with low LD show higher DIF ($r = -0.36$, $p = 0.006$), consistent with DIF capturing items sensitive to capability evolution.

Structural constraints and limitations are discussed in the Limitations section.

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

These findings motivate a concrete benchmark maintenance protocol. The minimum viable DIF audit requires only a binary response matrix and temporal cohort labels; no IRT model fitting is needed. We recommend: (1)~\emph{monitoring frequency}: run a full MH-DIF audit each quarter or whenever a new model cohort ($N \geq 80$) becomes available, using cross-subject matching as the default stratification; (2)~\emph{decision rules}: benchmark-wide C\% $> 20\%$ triggers item-level review; items flagged ETS~C in two consecutive audits are labeled ``unstable''; (3)~\emph{graduated responses}: (a)~flag-and-annotate (publish item stability labels alongside releases), (b)~item rotation (replace persistently unstable items with matched-difficulty alternatives), or (c)~weighted scoring (reduce unstable item weights in aggregate scores).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Applying Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we find that 31\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations under ETS Category~C classification. The instability is robust across thresholds (10--49\% at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \in [2.5, 1.0]$), confirmed non-random by a random-split control (0.18\%), and predominantly item-specific (92.4\%). This does not invalidate MMLU as a ranking instrument ($\rho \approx 0.997$) but signals measurement drift. Web-overlap contamination labels show near-independence with DIF flags (enrichment $\approx 0.96$), exposing a validation methodology gap; our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$, $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$) provides a controlled alternative, confirmed on GSM8K.

DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative information ($z = -8\sigma$ to $-49\sigma$ vs.\ random removal, Jaccard with high-discrimination items $= 0.12$) and exhibit a structured difficulty--direction interaction ($\rho = -0.597$): capability gains concentrate on hard items while easy items erode. DIF is a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter. LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring---periodic DIF audits, item-level stability labels, and cross-subject matching---alongside score reporting.

